WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.200
The Wizarding World exists in secret.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:07.649
That much is made clear early on in the Harry
Potter series, and the conflict that exists

00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:12.670
between wizards and non-magic users, known
as Muggles, is a constant focus throughout

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:17.810
all seven of the Harry Potter books, the eight
film adaptations, and the ongoing Fantastic

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.280
Beasts film franchise.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:24.300
However, despite how integral the rift between
the magical and Muggle communities is in the

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.070
series, neither the books nor the movies go
all that in depth into the history of the

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.290
conflict between the world's magical and non-magical
citizens.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.750
In fact, the Harry Potter series more or less
leaves the details of the relationship between

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:40.770
the muggle and wizarding societies to the
imagination.

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.730
The series refers to a handful of real-life
historical events, like the Salem Witch Trials

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:50.690
and the first and second world wars, in passing
from time to time, but it doesn't dive too

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:55.840
deeply into how the wizarding community eventually
achieved the secret existence that it maintains

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.750
throughout both franchises.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:02.580
One popular Harry Potter fan theory, however,
offers up a game-changing explanation for

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.000
the relationship between the Muggle and wizarding
communities.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.490
One Reddit user believes that the reason wizards
were forced into hiding is because they lost

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.260
a war with the Muggles hundreds of years prior
to the events of the Harry Potter series.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.810
This Redditor speculates that there may have
been a war between wizards and Muggles at

00:01:18.810 --> 00:01:24.350
some point during the Middle Ages, and the
Muggles, somehow, emerged as the victors.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:29.110
That defeat could, therefore, be what led
the wizarding community to not only live in

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:32.159
secret, but also in legitimate fear of Muggles.

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:36.479
It may even explain why the Minister of Magic
must keep the British Prime Minister up to

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:39.430
date on all matters within the Wizarding World.

00:01:39.430 --> 00:01:44.880
That is to say that the Minister doesn't necessarily
do it out of courtesy, but because it's been

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.740
a requirement ever since the Muggles scored
their decisive victory over wizards centuries

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:51.740
prior.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:56.770
It's a compelling theory, and one that totally
redefines the perceived power balance between

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.920
the wizards and Muggles of the world.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:03.799
It raised the possibility that Muggles may
have been far more capable of taking on wizards

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.970
in the Middle Ages than most Harry Potter
fans would likely assume.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:14.650
"I was the only one to see her for what she
was: A freak."

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:19.719
If this theory is true, it would certainly
explain some elements of the oddly strained

00:02:19.719 --> 00:02:22.319
relationship between Muggles and wizards.

00:02:22.319 --> 00:02:27.099
Beyond that, it might also explain why wizards
go to such extreme lengths to hide themselves

00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:29.349
from their non-magical brethren.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:33.730
Throughout the series, readers and audiences
learn that wizards cast spells more or less

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.500
constantly to cover their tracks from Muggles,
whether they're hiding entire castles — like

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:42.620
Hogwarts — or dressing in regular Muggle
clothing at the Quidditch World Cup to look

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:43.970
less suspicious.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:49.389
Even so, wizards take a lot of risks by stationing
important buildings or vital passageways like

00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:53.519
the pub that leads to Diagon Alley right in
the middle of London — though they seem

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:56.549
to bank on the fact that Muggles just don't
notice.

00:02:56.549 --> 00:03:01.680
"Excuse me sir, can you tell me where I might
find Platform 9 and three quarters?"

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.040
As the Knight Bus conductor Stan Shunpike
tells Harry in Prisoner of Azkaban, Muggles

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:11.359
don't see the super-fast, shape-shifting bus
moving at warp speeds throughout London because

00:03:11.359 --> 00:03:13.579
they simply aren't paying attention.

00:03:13.579 --> 00:03:17.230
There's another element of the wizarding world
that could definitely explain the ongoing

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:22.469
animosity between Muggles and wizards: specifically,
the belief by certain sects of wizards that

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:25.330
Muggleborn wizards are inferior.

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:29.709
Muggleborns — meaning witches and wizards
born to non-magical parents who unexpectedly

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:34.489
show magical abilities — are equally as
powerful as their pureblood brethren, as witches

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:37.830
like Lily Potter and Hermione Granger handily
prove.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:43.059
However, pureblood purists like Draco Malfoy
often level slurs like "Mudblood" against

00:03:43.059 --> 00:03:45.889
their Muggleborn and "half-blood" counterparts.

00:03:45.889 --> 00:03:50.819
Don't worry — Hermione gets her revenge
against Draco time and time again.

00:03:50.819 --> 00:03:51.930
"Aww."

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.249
"Malfoy, you ok?"

00:03:55.249 --> 00:04:00.109
This absurd prejudice could be related to
a war in which Muggles bested wizards; perhaps

00:04:00.109 --> 00:04:04.879
ancient wizarding families like the Malfoys
are still bitter that their non-magical counterparts

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:06.870
somehow beat them in battle.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:10.829
Obviously, none of this has ever been confirmed
in the series, but it's always fun to wonder

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:17.030
if, at one point, Muggles scored a huge victory
over a bunch of super-powerful witches.

